WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:06.106
In this video you will read the water data of a
whole area, learn to read a falling water level,

00:00:06.226 --> 00:00:09.082
and compare stations with statistics.

00:00:09.883 --> 00:00:13.843
Anyone in OurWater can read the water data of
a whole area.

00:00:13.963 --> 00:00:17.755
First, switch the menu from Easy to Advanced.

00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:21.631
Under All monitoring data are two pages.

00:00:21.751 --> 00:00:24.535
Data trend draws lines over time.

00:00:24.655 --> 00:00:27.127
Data statistics gives numbers.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.830
Data trend starts empty on purpose.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.422
Open the Monitoring area box.

00:00:33.542 --> 00:00:36.158
An area is a group of stations.

00:00:36.983 --> 00:00:39.167
Choose Badgaon village.

00:00:39.287 --> 00:00:41.855
Each coloured line is one station.

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:47.063
Time runs along the bottom, and the
measurement, with its unit, up the side.

00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:50.259
Move the pointer over the chart.

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:53.763
A box shows each station's value on that date.

00:00:53.883 --> 00:00:56.259
The legend names each colour.

00:00:57.083 --> 00:01:04.187
To narrow down, use Station for one station, here
Asha's open well, or Station type for a whole kind,

00:01:04.307 --> 00:01:06.299
such as all wells.

00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:09.373
Data type is what is measured.

00:01:09.493 --> 00:01:14.293
For a well it is Groundwater depth, the
distance from the ground down to the water.

00:01:15.117 --> 00:01:16.989
Now read the line.

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:22.749
The number is how far down you reach to touch the
water, so a bigger number means lower water.

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:25.821
That is why the axis is upside down.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:30.850
So when the line slides down the page, the
water is falling.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.898
Here it sank for months, then climbed back.

00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:39.277
Open Analysis controls and untick Flip Y axis
if you prefer.

00:01:39.397 --> 00:01:42.493
A falling level then looks like a rising line.

00:01:43.317 --> 00:01:45.189
Tick it again.

00:01:45.309 --> 00:01:49.461
Granularity groups measurements by week, month
or year.

00:01:49.581 --> 00:01:51.501
Auto chooses for you.

00:01:52.317 --> 00:01:55.605
Series draws a line per station or an average.

00:01:55.725 --> 00:01:58.317
Measure can show the rate of change.

00:01:58.437 --> 00:02:01.941
Chart switches line, bars and areas.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.822
Chart stations picks which stations are drawn.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:10.490
Preprocess can smooth wiggles or clip extreme
values.

00:02:11.317 --> 00:02:16.141
Time range sets how far back to look, from the
last month to fifteen years.

00:02:16.261 --> 00:02:18.757
Here, the last six months.

00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:22.943
Custom range lets you type your own two dates.

00:02:23.063 --> 00:02:26.759
If nothing was measured between them, the page
says so.

00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:34.063
Under the chart are the numbers: the latest measurement,
the average, and the median, the middle value.

00:02:34.183 --> 00:02:39.199
Then the range, the count, how many stations
have data, and the dates.

00:02:40.017 --> 00:02:45.465
Show map opens a map of the same stations, so
you see where each line comes from.

00:02:46.283 --> 00:02:48.155
Happy with a picture?

00:02:48.275 --> 00:02:50.867
Press Save view and type a name.

00:02:51.683 --> 00:02:54.371
Press Enter to keep it in your account.

00:02:54.491 --> 00:02:57.371
Reset clears the page back to the start.

00:02:58.183 --> 00:03:03.631
Pick one from Load a saved view and the picture
comes straight back, here a ready-made one,

00:03:03.751 --> 00:03:07.111
with its area, station and settings.

00:03:07.917 --> 00:03:11.829
Now Data statistics: the same area as numbers.

00:03:11.949 --> 00:03:14.565
Choose the area and a Time range.

00:03:14.685 --> 00:03:17.445
Last 15 years counts everything.

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:24.394
Scope overview: how many stations, how many
measurements, how many stations have data,

00:03:24.514 --> 00:03:26.410
and the dates they cover.

00:03:27.217 --> 00:03:32.497
Data-type insights change with what you
measure: the shallowest depth, the deepest,

00:03:32.617 --> 00:03:34.873
the middle value and the spread.

00:03:35.683 --> 00:03:39.643
The Station value map colours each station
from low to high.

00:03:39.763 --> 00:03:45.403
Map value chooses latest, average, median,
minimum or maximum.

00:03:46.217 --> 00:03:49.889
The histogram counts measurements in each band
of values.

00:03:50.009 --> 00:03:52.649
The tall bar is where most of them sit.

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:55.994
The box plot shows the middle half.

00:03:56.114 --> 00:04:03.386
The box runs from Q1 to Q3: a quarter of
values lie below Q1, a quarter above Q3.

00:04:04.217 --> 00:04:09.593
Monthly data availability: each row is a
station, each column a month.

00:04:09.713 --> 00:04:13.721
Dark green means many measurements, striped
means none.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:20.526
Data quality has four checks: stations without
data, stations quiet for ninety days,

00:04:20.646 --> 00:04:24.582
entries at the same time, and statistical
outliers.

00:04:25.383 --> 00:04:28.503
An outlier is a value far from the rest.

00:04:28.623 --> 00:04:34.023
It may not be a mistake: the borewell is deeper and
the check-dam shallower than the well.

00:04:34.143 --> 00:04:36.231
Check before removing it.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.674
The Station comparison table lists every
station.

00:04:40.794 --> 00:04:45.714
Click a column name, such as Variability, to
rank the stations by it.

00:04:46.517 --> 00:04:48.413
Click again to reverse.

00:04:48.533 --> 00:04:54.269
Dashes mean no measurements of this type: the
rain gauge records rain, not water level.

00:04:55.083 --> 00:04:57.819
These pages have no download button.

00:04:57.939 --> 00:05:00.747
To keep a picture, use Save view.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:07.838
To take measurements away as a spreadsheet, open a
station's history in My measurements and press Export.

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:11.458
That is how to read the data of an area.

00:05:11.578 --> 00:05:14.242
Thanks for helping look after our water.
